{"content_hash": "e15dd51ab3040ab3", "version_id": 296, "article_id": 30, "level_code": "ja_n5", "lang": "ja", "level": 1, "level_label": "N5 初級", "title": "北極の土を木ではだんにして、あたたかくする", "title_zh": "用木头给北极冻土盖\"毯子\"保暖", "has_translation": true, "topic": "science", "source": "nature", "source_url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-03045-8", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.rawpixel.com/image_1300/cHJpdmF0ZS9sci9pbWFnZXMvd2Vic2l0ZS8yMDIyLTA0L2ZsNDg0MTYwMDkzMjYtaW1hZ2Uta3A0dndxanUuanBn.jpg", "credit": "U.S. Department of Energy · cc0 1.0 · via rawpixel"}, "published_date": "2026-10-08", "word_count": 400, "reading_minutes": 1.3, "lead": "北極がだんだんあたたかくなっています。こおりの土がとけて、ガスが出ます。カナダの会社が、木ではだんを作りました。", "lead_zh": "北极正在慢慢变暖。冰冻的土壤融化后会释放气体。加拿大一家公司用木头做了一种\"毯子\"。", "preview": "北極の土は、こおりのようにかたいです。これをえいえいこおりの土と言います。あたたかくなると、この土はとけます。すると、ちきゅうおんだんかのガスが出ます。このガスは、日本と同じくらい多いです。", "preview_zh": "北极的土壤像冰一样硬，这叫永久冻土。天气变暖时，这些土会融化。然后就会释放出导致全球变暖的气体。这些气体和日本排放的一样多。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 286, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@8760f31/content/audio/30/ja_n5.mp3", "duration": 86.987, "size_bytes": 525600, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "ja-JP-NanamiNeural", "word_count": 260, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@8760f31/content/data/versions/296.json", "paragraphs": ["北極の土は、こおりのようにかたいです。これをえいえいこおりの土と言います。あたたかくなると、この土はとけます。すると、ちきゅうおんだんかのガスが出ます。このガスは、日本と同じくらい多いです。", "カナダの会社が、新しいほうほうをためしました。木くずと土とたねをまぜます。そして、やしの実のマットをかぶせます。これを、とけている土のふちに置きます。すると、土がくずれにくくなります。", "ことしの夏、ためしたところを見ました。はだんのないところは、5メートルくずれました。はだんのあるところは、くずれませんでした。これは、とてもよいけっかです。", "でも、まだわかりません。一つだけのためです。一つだけの夏です。もっとたくさん、ためすひつようがあります。", "この会社は、大きくなりたいです。10年で、2万か所に置きたいです。そうすると、ハンガリーと同じくらいのガスをへらせます。会社は、お金をあつめています。1.4カナダドルです。", "このほうほうで、ちきゅうのガスをへらせるかもしれません。でも、もっと研究がひつようです。"], "paragraphs_zh": ["北极的土壤像冰一样硬，这叫永久冻土。天气变暖时，这些土会融化。然后就会释放出导致全球变暖的气体。这些气体和日本排放的一样多。", "一家加拿大公司试验了新方法。把木屑、泥土和种子混在一起，再盖上椰子壳做的垫子。把它放在正在融化的土壤边缘，土壤就不容易崩塌了。", "今年夏天，他们去看了试验的地方。没有盖\"毯子\"的地方塌了5米。盖了\"毯子\"的地方没有塌。这是很好的结果。", "但是，现在还说不准。只试了一个地方，只试了一个夏天。需要做更多试验。", "这家公司想做大。他们想在10年里铺到2万个地方。这样就能减少和匈牙利一样多的气体。公司正在筹钱，已经筹到140万加元。", "这个方法也许能减少地球上的气体。但是还需要更多研究。"], "body": "北極の土は、こおりのようにかたいです。これをえいえいこおりの土と言います。あたたかくなると、この土はとけます。すると、ちきゅうおんだんかのガスが出ます。このガスは、日本と同じくらい多いです。\n\nカナダの会社が、新しいほうほうをためしました。木くずと土とたねをまぜます。そして、やしの実のマットをかぶせます。これを、とけている土のふちに置きます。すると、土がくずれにくくなります。\n\nことしの夏、ためしたところを見ました。はだんのないところは、5メートルくずれました。はだんのあるところは、くずれませんでした。これは、とてもよいけっかです。\n\nでも、まだわかりません。一つだけのためです。一つだけの夏です。もっとたくさん、ためすひつようがあります。\n\nこの会社は、大きくなりたいです。10年で、2万か所に置きたいです。そうすると、ハンガリーと同じくらいのガスをへらせます。会社は、お金をあつめています。1.4カナダドルです。\n\nこのほうほうで、ちきゅうのガスをへらせるかもしれません。でも、もっと研究がひつようです。", "body_zh": "北极的土壤像冰一样硬，这叫永久冻土。天气变暖时，这些土会融化。然后就会释放出导致全球变暖的气体。这些气体和日本排放的一样多。\n\n一家加拿大公司试验了新方法。把木屑、泥土和种子混在一起，再盖上椰子壳做的垫子。把它放在正在融化的土壤边缘，土壤就不容易崩塌了。\n\n今年夏天，他们去看了试验的地方。没有盖\"毯子\"的地方塌了5米。盖了\"毯子\"的地方没有塌。这是很好的结果。\n\n但是，现在还说不准。只试了一个地方，只试了一个夏天。需要做更多试验。\n\n这家公司想做大。他们想在10年里铺到2万个地方。这样就能减少和匈牙利一样多的气体。公司正在筹钱，已经筹到140万加元。\n\n这个方法也许能减少地球上的气体。但是还需要更多研究。", "text": "北極の土は、こおりのようにかたいです。これをえいえいこおりの土と言います。あたたかくなると、この土はとけます。すると、ちきゅうおんだんかのガスが出ます。このガスは、日本と同じくらい多いです。 カナダの会社が、新しいほうほうをためしました。木くずと土とたねをまぜます。そして、やしの実のマットをかぶせます。これを、とけている土のふちに置きます。すると、土がくずれにくくなります。 ことしの夏、ためしたところを見ました。はだんのないところは、5メートルくずれました。はだんのあるところは、くずれませんでした。これは、とてもよいけっかです。 でも、まだわかりません。一つだけのためです。一つだけの夏です。もっとたくさん、ためすひつようがあります。 この会社は、大きくなりたいです。10年で、2万か所に置きたいです。そうすると、ハンガリーと同じくらいのガスをへらせます。会社は、お金をあつめています。1.4カナダドルです。 このほうほうで、ちきゅうのガスをへらせるかもしれません。でも、もっと研究がひつようです。", "vocab": [{"word": "北極", "pos": "noun", "zh": "北极", "note": "ほっきょく，地球最北的地方"}, {"word": "土", "pos": "noun", "zh": "土壤", "note": "つち"}, {"word": "とける", "pos": "verb", "zh": "融化", "note": "冰或冻土变成水"}, {"word": "ガス", "pos": "noun", "zh": "气体", "note": "这里指二氧化碳等温室气体"}, {"word": "木くず", "pos": "noun", "zh": "木屑", "note": "きくず，碎木头"}, {"word": "はだん", "pos": "noun", "zh": "毯子", "note": "这里指盖在土上的保温层"}, {"word": "くずれる", "pos": "verb", "zh": "崩塌", "note": "土或山塌下来"}, {"word": "へらす", "pos": "verb", "zh": "减少", "note": "让数量变少"}], "title_original": "Woody ‘blankets’ are being deployed on Arctic permafrost to reduce carbon emissions", "summary_original": "Nature, Published online: 08 October 2026;\ndoi:10.1038/d41586-026-03045-8\nA start-up company is testing whether stabilizing areas of rapidly eroding permafrost can prevent greenhouse-gas emissions.", "body_original": "NEWS\n08 October 2026\nWoody ‘blankets’ are being deployed on Arctic permafrost to reduce carbon emissions\nA start-up company is testing whether stabilizing areas of rapidly eroding permafrost can prevent greenhouse-gas emissions.\nBy\nJames Dinneen\nJames Dinneen\nView author publications\nSearch author on:\nPubMed\nGoogle Scholar\nEmail\nBluesky\nFacebook\nLinkedIn\nReddit\nWhatsapp\nX\nSave article\nView saved research\nA Canadian start-up firm is developing a way to prevent greenhouse-gas emissions from permafrost by stabilizing the soil with an insulating ‘blanket’ of woody materials. If the method works, it could be the first tool to directly forestall green-house gas emissions that are escaping from permafrost as the Arctic heats.\n“Our goal is to make sure the carbon in the soil remains buried,” says Cansu Culha, co-founder of PermaC and a natural-hazards researcher at the University of British Columbia in Vancouver, Canada.\nEarth’s\nfrozen environments are thawing\nas temperatures rise in response to human emissions. The only reliable way to slow that melting on a global scale is to slash emissions from the burning of fossil fuels. But some researchers are exploring methods to prevent the thaw directly, ranging from placing reflective mats on glaciers to pumping water onto sea ice to thicken it.\nResearchers say that PermaC’s approach is the first they know of to focus on preventing permafrost thaw and its\nassociated emissions\n, which are estimated to be equivalent to those of Japan.\nThe company just completed the first test of its insulating blankets in Canada’s Northwest Territories at an area of rapidly eroding permafrost known as a retrogressive thaw slump. These slumps occur when a hill of permafrost thaws and collapses, enabling erosion to take big bites out of the landscape. This exposes deeper layers of soil to warming, forming hotspots of CO\n2\nand methane emissions. One estimate suggests that these areas of rapid erosion could amplify permafrost emissions by as much as 40% compared to models that only account for gradual thaw.\nThe company’s approach is to stabilize the edge of the slumps with a layer of wood shavings, soil and native seeds, and a burlap-like mat of coconut husks. Culha says she got the idea after hearing about Indigenous communities in Alaska that prevent permafrost degradation by planting trees along the edges of slumps. Similar erosion-control blankets are used in temperate areas, but they haven’t been used to insulate or stabilize permafrost until now, she says.\nPermaC has yet to release results from this year'stest. But the blanket seems to have halted erosion: over the Arctic summer, the edge of the slump without the blanket retreated by 5 metres, whereas the blanketed area saw no measurable retreat, Culha says.\n“That’s very promising,” says Christina Schaedel, a permafrost scientist at the Woodwell Climate Research Center in Falmouth, Massachusetts, who has been advising the company. However, the result is from only one slump over one summer, she adds.\nGreen blanket\nOne challenge is calculating how much CO\n2\nthe blankets prevent from being emitted. Measuring the CO\n2\nemissions from slumps is not straightforward and remains an active research question, says Mark Lara, a biologist who studies permafrost landscapes at the University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign. “There definitely are slumps that are spewing out carbon,” he says. But there are “lots of minutiae that could dramatically impact the results”, he adds. For instance, the emissions from any given slump depend on its geometry, the amount of carbon in the soil and how much of that carbon ends up as CO\n2\nor methane after erosion.\nSo far, Culha has only rough estimates for the emissions that the blankets might prevent. Extrapolating from research on erosion rates and soil carbon in the Arctic, she thinks that about 2,200 tonnes of CO\n2\na year might be prevented from being emitted at the average carbon-rich slump. If those calculations are accurate and the blankets can be installed at 20,000 slumps across Canada and Alaska — the company’s ambitious goal over the next decade — she estimates that they might prevent about 40 million tonnes of CO\n2\nemissions a year, roughly equivalent to the annual emissions of Hungary.\nCulha says the company is developing a detailed methodology that a third party could use to verify prevented emissions, which would be necessary for the company to sell carbon credits for emissions the blankets prevent. People could purchase those credits to offset emissions elsewhere. The firm aims to issue credits as soon as next year, and has so far raised Can$1.4 million (US$1 million) from investors, says Culha.\nEnjoying our latest content?\nLog in or create an account to continue\nAccess the most recent journalism from Nature's award-winning team\nExplore the latest features & opinion covering groundbreaking research\nAccess through your institution\nor\nSign in or create an account\nContinue with Google\nContinue with ORCiD\ndoi: https://doi.org/10.1038/d41586-026-03045-8\nReprints and permissions\nRelated Articles\nEpic blazes threaten Arctic permafrost. Can firefighters save it?\nThis Arctic town wants to make renewable energy work at the top of the world\nIs lightning striking the Arctic more than ever before?\nThe Arctic is burning like never before — and that’s bad news for climate change\nSubjects\nClimate change\nEngineering\nEnvironmental sciences\nLatest on:\nClimate change\nEngineering\nEnvironmental sciences\nAustralia’s bush fires got worse after British colonization — this is why\nNews\n07 OCT 26\nThe Great Barrier Reef requires special attention in the developing super El Niño\nCorrespondence\n06 OCT 26\nNature has rights — it is time the world recognized them\nComment\n02 OCT 26\nA 7-eV bandgap semiconductor based on silicon-doped α-(AlxGa1−x)2O3\nArticle\n07 OCT 26\nA quadruped robot designed to complete a marathon on a single battery charge\nArticle\n23 SEP 26\nAnodic Pd membrane H2 extraction enhances thermochemical dehydrogenation\nArticle\n09 SEP 26\nAustralia’s current wildfire crisis linked to colonial land-use change\nArticle\n07 OCT 26\nNature has rights — it is time the world recognized them\nComment\n02 OCT 26\nAgricultural liming is a carbon sink in the Mississippi River Basin\nArticle\n23 SEP 26\nJobs\nFaculty Positions at the Center for Machine Learning Research (CMLR), Peking University\nCMLR's goal is to advance machine learning-related research across a wide range of disciplines.\nBeijing (CN)\nCenter for Machine Learning Research (CMLR), Peking University\nAssistant or Associate Professor of Cell Biology\nDepartment of Cell Biology at the University of Virginia School of Medicine invites applications for Assistant or Associate Professor of Cell Biology\nCharlottesville, Virginia\nUniversity of Virginia\nRecruitment Announcement for High-Level Talent at the Center for Agricultural Microbiology\nJoin HZAU's global faculty team to advance research with competitive benefits.\nWuhan, Hubei (CN)\nHuazhong Agricultural University (HZAU)\nOpen-Rank Faculty Position in Theoretical Biology\nThe Department of Biological Sciences seeks an outstanding individual for an open-rank faculty position in Theoretical Biology.\nNashville, Tennessee\nVanderbilt University Department of Biological Sciences\nProteomics Specialist\nBuild the science that shapes the future of human health. Application closing date: 08 November 2026 Join a place where ambitious science thrives...\nMilan (IT)\nHuman Technopole", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T12:42:12.257593+00:00", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "